{"results":[{"url":"https://nebraska.tv/news/local/property-owners-fuming-at-valuation-increases-assessor-says-changes-reflect-market","final_url":"https://nebraska.tv/news/local/property-owners-fuming-at-valuation-increases-assessor-says-changes-reflect-market","title":"Property owners fuming at valuation increases, assessor says changes reflect market","description":"Property owners are fuming at the prospect of higher taxes in a community hit hard by the virus. \"Given a 100 year pandemic that's going on. I can't believe we chose the year to do this,\" said Andy Marsh of Keystone Properties, which runs the Continental Gardens apartments in Grand Island. A day after helping five tenants apply for relief funds to cover their rent, Marsh went before the Hall County Board.","language":"en","text":"# Property owners fuming at valuation increases, assessor says changes reflect market\n\n---\n\nby Steve White\n\nTue, June 9, 2020 at 6:16 PM\n\nUpdated Fri, June 12, 2020 at 5:08 PM\n\n3VIEW ALL PHOTOS\n\nKristi Wold Hall County Assessor speaks to county commissioners June 9, 2020 (NTV News)\n\nGRAND ISLAND, Neb — Property owners are fuming at the prospect of higher taxes in a community hit hard by the virus.\n\n\"Given a 100 year pandemic that's going on. I can't believe we chose the year to do this,\" said Andy Marsh of Keystone Properties, which runs the Continental Gardens apartments in Grand Island.\n\nA day after helping five tenants apply for relief funds to cover their rent, Marsh went before the Hall County Board.\n\nHe said his taxes will likely go up tens of thousands of dollars, even hundreds of thousands, which could force him to raise rent, after the county raised the valuation on his property.\n\n\"143 percent increase on one property in our organization,\" Marsh told NTV.\n\nMarsh was one of several property owners to speak up Tuesday.\n\nHall County Assessor Kristi Wold said it's important to understand they base valuations on market conditions January First.\n\n\"Nebraska is a market state so if your neighbors are selling and buying high, unfortunately it affects you,\" she said following the meeting.\n\nAnd while the pandemic has had an impact, homes continue to sell.\n\n\"Our house sales have not slowed at all even during COVID,\" Wold said.\n\nSome county commissioners said home owners shouldn't focus on the increase, but think about what their home would sell for, and the valuation should be close.\n\n\"If you would not sell it for the price on it now, file a protest,\" Wold said. \"We have referees in to help with those, with make appointments, it's a smooth process but you have to have evidence why your value's too high.\"\n\nOther commissioners said the county needs to adapt to changing economics and find a way to provide relief.\n\nWold said valuations in Hall County are on the low end of what they can be, and said her office follows state guidelines.\n\nAndy Marsh said that may be, but he thinks any increase should be done incrementally, instead of a hit when his tenants can lease afford it.\n\n\"One of our properties did not increase 3.7 million dollars in one year, and now to fix the county's problem in one year, not fair to us,\" he said.\n\nMarsh said he'd have to raise rent $40-50, which could mean a decision between food and medicine or rent. Marsh said he has his finger on the pulse, as a landlord and also a board member for the Hope Harbor homeless shelter.\n\nThe county assessor says if you plan to protest it helps to have documentation or a recent appraisal. Forms are available online.","author":"Steve White","published_date":"2020-06-09T23:16:41.000Z","latency_ms":2148.092721006833,"format":"markdown"}],"errors":[]}